BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"North Bay Bohemian": "https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-frank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition"}
X-SOURCE-ID:bohemian
SUMMARY:Henry Frank: J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260927T170000
UID:260905OmwvpXSVDkuyou6d373uRA
DESCRIPTION:The San Geronimo Valley Community Center is proud to present f
 eatured September artist **Henry Frank** in the Del Mue Galleries this Se
 ptember. There will be a reception for the artist as part of the Internati
 onal Day of Peace Festival Finale on **September 20 from 2 – 6 pm**.\n\n
 **J-80928: A Human Experience Exhibition** explores Henry Frank’s trans
 formative journey through nearly three decades of incarceration in the Cal
 ifornia Department of Corrections. Through deeply personal artwork\, the e
 xhibition reflects on trauma\, friendship\, identity\, hope\, and personal
  growth while inviting viewers to recognize the humanity and capacity for 
 change in people who are incarcerated.\n\n**ARTISTS STATEMENT** **J-80928:
  A Human Experience Exhibition** explores my personal journey serving a 29
 -year-to-life sentence in the California Department of Corrections as an I
 ndigenous person\, an artist\, and a human being. I was incarcerated from 
 February 15\, 1994\, to April 19\, 2013\, and during much of that time\, I
  lived hour to hour. For the first 14 years\, my mantras were: expect the 
 worst and hope for the best\, trust no one except myself\, and fear is a s
 tate of mind. While many experiences were deeply traumatic\, there were al
 so moments of humanity\, compassion\, joy\, and empathy that transformed h
 ow I saw myself and the world around me.\n\nThe last six years of my incar
 ceration became the most transformative period of my life. I began to beli
 eve that I would not die in prison. I found hope\, discovered the importan
 ce of serving others\, began to see myself as a serious artist\, and gain
 ed a deeper understanding of my place in the world. My mantras evolved int
 o: expect the best and be prepared for anything less\, trust everyone unti
 l they give me a reason not to\, and happiness is a choice. During this ti
 me\, I learned to love and respect myself\, to be accountable\, to live in
  the present\, to be a true friend\, and to serve others with intention an
 d compassion.\n\nThis exhibition is an oeuvre dedicated to the experiences
 \, emotional trauma\, friendships\, and reclamation of identity that shape
 d me throughout my incarceration. Through this body of work\, I hope peopl
 e can connect with parts of my journey and recognize that I am not so diff
 erent from them. More importantly\, I want to humanize those who are curre
 ntly incarcerated and raise awareness that people are capable of change. T
 hey can become contributing members of their communities. They have famili
 es\, hobbies\, hopes\, dreams\, and the capacity for growth and transforma
 tion.\n\nI have been working toward completing this body of work and plan 
 to have the exhibition finished completely by the end of this year.\n\nThi
 s exhibition is partially funded by an Artist Fellowship Grant from the Ar
 t for Justice Fund. I am grateful for their support and patience for this 
 exhibition to evolve into its fullest potential.\n\nHuman Being\n\nHenry E
 dward Frank\n\nYurok and Kashaya Pomo
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
URL:https://sangeronimovalleycommunitycenter.eventcalendarapp.com/henry-fr
 ank-j-80928-a-human-experience-exhibition
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
